      "account": "ONNEL LAB (onnel-lab-b5b9b0421)",
      "draft_sha256": "7eeb55a4f5d794ef1f8bcd7f10cbc66715bb0ec9ffc61d24d812495a7f150c3a",
      "posted_body_sha256": "3200f6aa091162cc1ebb7b07e688e19e168c2817181e31c1bfb80e0361b43ba2",
      "draft_hash_normalization": "UTF-8 with CRLF normalized to LF",
      "posted_body_hash_normalization": "Submitted UTF-8 source with CRLF normalized to LF; terminal LF removed; before platform URL shortening",
      "submitted_body": "A reliable plan for how to organize audio files on Android and check that they play offline starts with the result and constraints.\n\nPreserve a separate copy of audio you have the right to use, choose a clear folder and browsing structure, and check a small library with Wi-Fi and mobile data disconnected before adding the rest. Verify that the expected files appear, play more than one track, and reopen the player while still offline.\n\nBefore changing tools:\n- Keep an untouched copy. Before moving, renaming, or editing audio, preserve another copy outside the working folder. If all copies are on the same phone, a second folder on the same phone will not protect against loss of that device. Confirm you can open the separate copy before treating it as a fallback.\n\nRead the full article:\nhttps://onnellab.com/blog/en/organize-local-music-library-offline-android/",
      "source_draft_path": "generated/social/linkedin/en/music/organize-local-music-library-offline-android.txt",
